    "executiveSummary": "Much like a basketball player maintaining one pivot foot firmly grounded on the court while scanning the floor for open passing lanes, modern professional reinvention requires a solid anchor before you move. Most professionals make the mistake of leaping into the void when restlessness strikes, abandoning accumulated skills and financial stability in search of an elusive passion. Jenny Blake deconstructs career mobility into an engineering feedback loop: you don't jump; you systematically shift weight from a rooted foundation of strengths and cash reserves into low-risk, calibrated tests.\n\nThink of the process as pruning and propagating in a garden rather than uprooting the entire orchard. In the Plant phase, you catalog your existing soil\u2014your core competencies, personal values, and runway. Next, in the Scan phase, you raise a periscope to map out peripheral trends, skill requirements, and helpful people without forcing premature commitments. Then, in the Pilot phase, you act like an agile product designer: you test miniature, low-overhead prototypes of your desired direction through weekend side-projects, micro-consulting, or 10-percent internal role shifts. Only when data proves an experiment viable do you pull the lever on Launch.\n\nBy treating career transition as an ongoing biological operating system rather than a mid-career crisis, you neutralize paralysis. Fear is simply an indicator of growth crossing into unfamiliar terrain. When your next move is treated as an empirical trial rather than a permanent life sentence, failure transforms from a personal indictment into useful diagnostic feedback, keeping your professional evolution fluid, resilient, and continuously self-renewing.",
