      "headline": "Fund Your Runway",
      "keyInsight": "Panic is a terrible career advisor. When your bank balance hits empty, your brain flips into survival mode. You stop thinking about clever ideas and start grabbing whatever pays fast. Jenny Blake points out a wild paradox here. To take bold, exciting leaps, you need deeply boring financial safety nets. A runway is not just cash sitting in an account. It is emotional oxygen. Every extra month of living expenses gives your mind permission to experiment without breaking into a cold sweat. If you jump off a cliff without savings, you are not being brave. You are just being careless. True reinvention needs steady fuel in the tank so you can steer calmly toward what works.",
      "content": "Think of career change like planning a long road trip across the desert. If your fuel gauge is flashing bright red, you cannot enjoy the scenic mountain route. You end up frantically pulling into the first sketchy gas station you see. Financial stress does the exact same thing to your decision making. It narrows your vision until you grab the nearest bad job offer just to pay rent.\n\nFunding your runway means figuring out your bare-bones survival number. What does it actually cost each month to keep the lights on and buy groceries? Strip away the fancy dinners and unused subscriptions for a moment. Once you know that base number, every dollar saved buys you literal days of creative freedom. Six months of savings equals six months of calm testing.\n\nMoney is only half of the fuel equation. Your physical energy and daily habits make up the other half. If you burn out working eighty hours a week, you have zero stamina left to explore new paths. Protect your runway by guarding your sleep, your savings, and your calendar. A solid cushion turns a terrifying leap into a calm walk across a sturdy bridge.",
